        "promptType": "define",
        "text": "Read the locations from Google Sheets.\nUse OpenWeatherMap to get the weather for each location.\n\nUse the calendar tool to check for any scheduled hiking events between now and 3 days from now. When calling the calendar tool, always provide both a start time and end time in ISO 8601 format (e.g. 2026-07-25T00:00:00+05:30) — never leave these blank. If no events are found, continue the report without calendar data rather than treating it as an error. If a hike is already scheduled, prioritize weather details for that specific day/trail in the report.\n\nCreate a concise weather report.\n\nFormat the report as clean HTML (not plain text) so it renders properly in Gmail. Follow this structure:\n- <h2> for the main title (\"Weather Report for Hyderabad Hiking Trails\")\n- <h3> subheadings for each section (e.g. \"Current Conditions\", \"Trail Recommendations\")\n- <ul><li> bullet lists for individual data points — never a single run-on paragraph\n- <strong> to bold key labels (e.g. temperature, humidity)\n- Group trail recommendations into two lists: \"Shady trails\" and \"Exposed trails\", based on the Shade Level column\n- A short closing <p> note about elevation/temperature caveats, wrapped in <em>\n- Do not include markdown syntax (no **, no ##, no === dividers) — HTML tags only\n\nSend it via Gmail, setting the email content type to HTML. When calling the Gmail tool, ensure the message content is valid within a JSON string — escape any double quotes and avoid unescaped special characters.",
